# 112 - SHORT-CIRCUIT MADE OBSERVABLE, and-form. §2/§6 (.40): `false and rhs`
# does not evaluate rhs, so f() is never called and never prints. The whole
# assertion is the ABSENCE of an "f called" line before `false`. This is the
# case most likely to catch a real divergence: the VM compiles and/or as a
# conditional jump over the rhs, while a tree-walker must special-case the
# node before recursing - two entirely different mechanisms for one rule.
--- source
fn f() {
  print "f called";
  return true;
}
print false and f();
--- expect
false
